<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  gateway-service.slnx

  The per service solution for the Gateway service: the sole ingress and composition root of the
  PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
    The mandated per service workflow is run from this directory, and every verb is invoked with no
    solution or project argument:

        cd services/gateway-service
        dotnet restore
        dotnet build -c Release
        dotnet test (collecting XPlat Code Coverage)

    A bare invocation resolves its target by finding exactly one solution file in the current
    directory. This file is that solution file. Enumerating Gateway's two projects here is the
    entire mechanism by which Gateway restores, builds and tests standalone from a clean checkout
    with no sibling service present, which is the property that makes this service independently
    deployable rather than a folder inside a monolith.

  FORMAT IS .slnx, NOT .sln
    .NET 10 emits the XML solution format: dotnet new sln produces a .slnx whose root element is
    Solution and whose members are Project elements carrying a relative Path attribute. That was
    verified against the pinned SDK on the machine that authored this file rather than assumed from
    documentation. The legacy .sln text format is not used anywhere in this repository, and the
    required path for this file carries the .slnx extension.

  NEVER ADD A SOLUTION FILTER, AND NEVER ADD A SECOND SOLUTION, TO THIS DIRECTORY
    Do not author a .slnf solution filter here. The planning record described legacy .slnf filters
    failing against a .slnx format solution with MSB4014 in all three verbs; that was MEASURED
    FALSE on the pinned SDK - see docs/BUILD.md section 2, which carries the measurement, the four
    situations that DO fail and the code's real trigger. The prohibition rests on the hand
    maintained project list instead, which is the durable reason. And a
    filter would be pointless here regardless: this solution has two members and neither of them is
    ever the one you would want to filter out. A second solution file is worse than pointless. With
    more than one solution in the directory the bare commands above stop working entirely and fail
    with MSB1011, "specify which project or solution file to use because this folder contains more
    than one project or solution file", which was reproduced deliberately on the pinned SDK to confirm
    the constraint is real rather than folklore. Exactly one solution file belongs in this
    directory, and it is this one.

  RELATIONSHIP TO THE REPOSITORY ROOT SOLUTION
    The repository root PowerFramework.slnx enumerates every project in the refactor, including this
    service's two. The two solutions coexist by design and neither supersedes the other: the root
    solution is the whole estate view used for a single pass build of everything, while this one is
    the independently buildable unit that CI and the container build exercise per service. They must
    always name the same two Gateway project paths, so renaming or relocating either project is a
    change to both files, plus the sibling Dockerfile, which copies and publishes these same paths
    from a repository root build context.

  EXACTLY TWO MEMBERS, AND EXACTLY THESE TWO

    PowerFramework.Gateway
      The application. Its own ProjectReference entries pull in the published contracts project and
      the shared libraries it consumes, so those arrive transitively and are deliberately NOT listed
      here. Solution membership is not how a project expresses a dependency, and restating the root
      solution's list would only create a second inventory to keep in step with the first.

    PowerFramework.Gateway.Tests
      The tests, and load bearing rather than a courtesy. This project is the only producer of
      Gateway's coverage.cobertura.xml, and that report is what the 80 percent line coverage per
      service gate is measured from. Leave it out of this solution and a bare test run in this
      directory discovers nothing, emits no report, and the gate cannot be evaluated at all, which
      is a failure that reads exactly like a pass.

    No sibling service project appears here. Gateway reaches DataServices, Persistence and Security
    over the network through the published contracts, never through a project reference, because a
    single solution spanning several services is precisely the collapsed outcome this decomposition
    exists to avoid.

    No deferred capability project appears here either. DesignSystem, Documents, Integration and
    ScriptBridge are outside this phase and have no project, no placeholder and no empty shell
    anywhere in this build graph. Their four reserved routes are route table metadata inside the
    Gateway application, and nothing compiles behind them.

    Both paths below are relative to this file's own directory, and neither rises above it.

  NOTHING IS CONFIGURED HERE
    No MSBuild property, no package version and no target framework appears in this file. The
    repository root supplies all of it by MSBuild directory walk up. Directory.Build.props sets
    net10.0, nullable reference types, implicit usings, warnings as errors and the analyzers, and
    turns on central package management. Directory.Packages.props holds every package version,
    including the two mandatory security pins that clear the NuGet audit advisories, and global.json
    pins the SDK. Restating any of that here would create a second source of truth able to drift
    this one service off a mandatory pin, which is the exact failure central package management
    exists to prevent. For the same reason this directory carries no per service
    Directory.Build.props, Directory.Packages.props, global.json, .editorconfig or .dockerignore.

  LEGACY REFERENCE (read only: never edited, never built, never shipped)
    The PowerBuilder workspace and target files were read as the conceptual source of the solution
    idea and for nothing else. The workspace file lists targets in much the way a solution lists
    projects, but the target it names loads its libraries as one flat ordered list into a single
    global namespace where symbol resolution depends on that ordering. A solution provides the
    opposite on purpose: independent compilation units with explicit references and no ordering
    sensitivity. The two PowerBuilder project objects were read for build intent only and are
    deliberately not translated, because they disagree with each other on library count and on
    vendor, one of them embeds developer workstation absolute paths together with a retired library
    name, and both name an image codec library that exists nowhere in the repository, so neither
    would build as written. No path in this file points at that tree, at any compiled PowerBuilder
    library, target or workspace, or at any native binary.
-->
<Solution>

  <Project Path="PowerFramework.Gateway/PowerFramework.Gateway.csproj" />
  <Project Path="PowerFramework.Gateway.Tests/PowerFramework.Gateway.Tests.csproj" />

</Solution>
